+
+The six earlier messages for deleted subscription 19714 and refund orders
+19759/19777/19795/19813/19860 share the same slt-paddle user, Basic-to-Pro flow,
+and provider-alignment cleanup semantics. The later explicit SW-05 fixture-cleanup
+mail makes their concurrent card-126 attribution strong, although the owner had not
+yet appended its exact per-record closeout note. They remain excluded from the
+authored lifecycle verdict rather than being silently treated as lifecycle mail.
+
+Fresh duplicate-meta standing check and new finding
+---------------------------------------------------
+- Fresh concurrent subscription 20114 is now cancelled but retained and auditable.
+  It has exactly one row and one distinct value for each watched gateway key:
+  _payment_gateway=arraysubs_paddle; _gateway_status=cancelled;
+  _gateway_customer_id=<redacted in this evidence>; _payment_method_last4=4242.
+  Therefore the mandatory fresh duplicate-gateway-meta check passes.
+- Relationship resolution is exact: parent order 20113's serialized value is [20114],
+  and subscription 20114 cross-checks to customer 352 and its parent 20113.
+- Separate data-integrity failure: authoritative HPOS table wp_wc_orders_meta has
+  two identical _subscription_ids rows for order 20113, IDs 18026 and 18027, while
+  compatibility wp_postmeta has one row (156639). WC_Order single-value reads hide
+  this, while all-value reads return both. The recent-order duplicate query found
+  no other order >=15800 with more than one HPOS relationship row.
